t can form a Chow with that T8. The Chow must be consecutive: so if the discarded T8, possible Chows are [T6,T7,T8] or [T7,T8,T9] or [T8,T9,T10] but no T10. So only [T6,T7,T8] or [T7,T8,T9]. I have T6 and T7, so I can form [T6,T7,T8] using discarded T8 and my T6 and one T7. So I do not use my own T8 from hand. So my own T8 stays in hand. So after Chi, my hand tiles are: I remove T6 and one T7, but I keep my original T8. So hand becomes: W2, W6, W7 (one left), W8, T5, T7 (one left), T8 (original). That's 7 tiles? Wait: before Chi, hand: W2, W6, W7 (2), W8, T5, T6, T7 (2), T8 = 10 tiles. After removing T6 and one T7, I have: W2, W6, W7 (1), W8, T5, T7 (1), T8 = 7 tiles. Then I add the new meld, so total tiles = 7 hand + 6 melded = 13? No, 7+6=13, but then I must discard one, so I'll have 6 hand + 6 melded = 12? That's wrong. Let's do step by step with counts:\n\nStart: Hand tiles count = 10. Meld tiles count = 3. Total = 13.\nAction: Chi T8. \n- I take the discarded T8 and place it on table with two tiles from my hand (T6 and one T7) to form a new meld of 3 tiles.\n- My hand tiles decrease by 2 (T6 and one T7), so hand count becomes 8.\n- I add the new meld to my melds, so meld count becomes 6.\n- Total tiles now: 8 hand + 6 melds = 14 tiles.\n- I must discard one tile from hand to reduce total to 13.\n- After discard, hand count = 7, meld count = 6, total = 13.\nSo final hand tiles: the remaining 7 tiles from the 8 after Chi, minus one discard. Those 8 tiles after Chi (before discard) are: W2, W6, W7 (1), W8, T5, T7 (1), T8 (original). Yes, 7 distinct tiles but W7 and T7 are singles, total count: W2(1), W6(1), W7(1), W8(1), T5(1), T7(1), T8(1) = 7 tiles. So after discard, I'll have 6 hand tiles.\n\nSo if I Chi, I get an extra meld, but I have to discard one tile. The advantage is I progress toward having three melds (B5 pung, T6-T7-T8 chow, and potentially W6-W7-W8 chow in hand). But I expose a meld, which might limit patterns like Concealed Hand. Also, the discarded